WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.006
(upbeat inspirational music)

00:00:03.006 --> 00:00:05.216
- [Narrator] Like many American cities,

00:00:05.216 --> 00:00:07.901
Albuquerque has tried very
hard to help its homeless

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:10.814
population get back on their feet,

00:00:10.814 --> 00:00:14.458
but one tactic, in particular,
seems to be working.

00:00:14.458 --> 00:00:17.125
It involves this guy, Will Cole.

00:00:18.415 --> 00:00:22.018
He drives around in a van
and approaches panhandlers

00:00:22.018 --> 00:00:23.502
with an offer.

00:00:23.502 --> 00:00:25.479
- You interested in working today?

00:00:25.479 --> 00:00:26.502
All right, come on.

00:00:26.502 --> 00:00:28.634
Hop in, let's go get it done.

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:29.967
Thank you, guys.

00:00:33.492 --> 00:00:35.234
- [Narrator] Our story is
about a city that decided

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:38.756
to tackle a chronic
problem with a simple idea

00:00:38.756 --> 00:00:42.836
that first sprang to life
inside the mayor's brain.

00:00:42.836 --> 00:00:44.518
- Hello, I'm Richard
Berry and I'm the mayor

00:00:44.518 --> 00:00:47.038
of Albuquerque, New Mexico.

00:00:47.038 --> 00:00:48.402
For so many years and so many decades,

00:00:48.402 --> 00:00:49.933
with homelessness, with panhandling,

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:51.794
it's always been about
buying somebody a bus ticket

00:00:51.794 --> 00:00:55.871
to the next city and I just
wanted to stop doing that.

00:00:55.871 --> 00:00:57.691
(telephone ringing)

00:00:57.691 --> 00:01:01.122
- The mayor called with a plan
to give jobs to panhandlers

00:01:01.122 --> 00:01:04.602
and I knew just the
person to lead the charge.

00:01:04.602 --> 00:01:06.854
- [Narrator] She was
thinking of Will Cole, who,

00:01:06.854 --> 00:01:09.086
at the time, was a security
guard at the shelter.

00:01:09.086 --> 00:01:11.096
In a van paid for by the city,

00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:14.846
Will leaves the homeless
shelter at 7:30 a.m.

00:01:15.866 --> 00:01:19.482
- We ride around the city,
we locate panhandlers

00:01:19.482 --> 00:01:22.859
at different work sites, we
approach them and ask them

00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:24.523
if they're willing to
work and, if they are,

00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:26.698
we take them to our work
site and we get the job done

00:01:26.698 --> 00:01:27.838
that we're assigned to.

00:01:27.838 --> 00:01:28.671
Thank you, Sir.

00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:30.133
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:32.050
- Good to see you guys.

00:01:33.647 --> 00:01:34.783
- [Will] All right.

00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:37.707
Either litter sweeps or pulling weeds,

00:01:37.707 --> 00:01:39.734
it's a city beautification program.

00:01:39.734 --> 00:01:41.368
That's what it's all about.

00:01:41.368 --> 00:01:42.940
- My name is Martin Ramirez.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.547
I've been homeless for almost two years.

00:01:44.547 --> 00:01:47.825
I've been working with
Will for around four months

00:01:47.825 --> 00:01:49.511
and he's helped me a lot.

00:01:49.511 --> 00:01:51.193
Good work, guys, good work.

00:01:51.193 --> 00:01:53.370
It makes me realize what I had before.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.597
(upbeat pleasant music)

00:01:56.597 --> 00:01:58.831
- We offer them a six hour shift of work

00:01:58.831 --> 00:02:01.031
and we pay them in cash at $9 an hour.

00:02:01.031 --> 00:02:03.719
All right, thank you for
the day and here you go.

00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:04.869
Thank you much.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:07.591
If I were an employer, I
would hire a whole bunch of

00:02:07.591 --> 00:02:09.359
our clients that we pick up.

00:02:09.359 --> 00:02:11.290
I think, a lot of time,
the situation they're in,

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.751
they don't get a chance and
I'm glad that we're able

00:02:13.751 --> 00:02:16.919
to give them that chance
and get them started.

00:02:16.919 --> 00:02:19.391
(whistling)

00:02:19.391 --> 00:02:22.119
- [Narrator] In just over
a year, Will has employed

00:02:22.119 --> 00:02:25.637
hundreds of people and they've
cleaned up 400 city blocks

00:02:25.637 --> 00:02:29.559
and removed over 120,000
pounds of litter and the idea

00:02:29.559 --> 00:02:32.279
has spread to six other cities,

00:02:32.279 --> 00:02:35.109
including Chicago and Denver.

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:38.299
Most importantly, dozens of
people, like Mike Whitaker here,

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:40.448
have found permanent jobs and housing

00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:43.111
after working with Will.

00:02:43.111 --> 00:02:45.730
- After I started working
on the Better Way program,

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.949
I got this job doing 25 hours a week and

00:02:48.949 --> 00:02:51.909
have an apartment of my own.

00:02:51.909 --> 00:02:53.989
He showed me I could show
up for work every day and

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:55.970
do the job and be sober.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.890
I got used to being around people again

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.551
and having conversations.

00:02:59.551 --> 00:03:00.599
Thank you, Will.

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:02.290
- [Narrator] And, to bring
this story full circle,

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.850
the mayor just came up with another idea.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:08.369
Will is this year's Good
Samaritan of Albuquerque.

00:03:08.369 --> 00:03:10.141
- It just shows you how a
little ripple can turn into

00:03:10.141 --> 00:03:13.861
a big wave and get a lot
of things going for people.

00:03:13.861 --> 00:03:15.568
You start small, but it gets bigger.

00:03:15.568 --> 00:03:19.318
(upbeat inspirational music)

